Z||000000||My7Fieros||09-10-2000||09:04 PM||||Well I sold a Fiero, the silver 86 Se V6 5spd. Not to worry, it went to a good friend, the same guy whos house I use to do the work on my Fieros!<BR>Anyway, its actually ok, Im wanting to replace it with a 4 banger. I dont have any 4 bangers and would like to have one because a.) better gas milage, would make a great "run around town" car..and b.) well, just so that I can say I have one!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Since Im fairly unfamiliar with them, can anyone tell me what they think an average price for one in very good condition, relativly low miles would cost? Pref. no 84s, but I guess if recall work has been done and its in good enough shape Id consider. <BR>Anyway, you 4banger guys and gals, lemme know of what I should expect to pay. Thanx!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever2||09-10-2000||09:35 PM||||My 88 was 3000 from 1st owner.  Had 60k,A/C broke, sagging headliner, running rough, radio bad.  Otherwise beautiful.  Oh yeah needed clutch after a few months.  Still I'm real glad I got it. ||||reg||9
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||09-11-2000||12:02 PM||||<BR>I have an 84 SE 4.10 has PL, PW, PM, clean interior (no rips in drivers seat - a major plus), dead paint, and either a leaking head gasket or cracked head, either way the car is fantastic.<P>Aside from the head problem, the only qualm I have with the 4-cylinder is the rpms.  With that 4.10 you can't get it much past 100 for your running at 4500 rpm.  As long as I run it around 65-75 the rpms and the noise from the engine is tolerable.  I will give it one major plus, with the 4.10 you can launch that Fiero with very little work and can eat several cars off the line.<P>I have noticed that my 86 v6 4-spd and my 84 4-cyl 4-spd get ruffly the same gas mileage, so if you want it for gas mileage, my suggestion is to get a 5-spd.<P>I have an 84 because I want a Fiero from every year, except 88 - those are just too expensive for me to work on.<P>Just my nickles worth of advice, since two cents would dictate a shorter length.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-11-2000||12:54 PM||||I think you'll have to pony up around $2000.  If all you want is a solid, complete car and don't mind doing some work to it and having it painted, I think you'll find one for around $500.  Add $500 for a Fiero that doesn't need work (clutch, brakes, etc).  Add another $500-$750 if you want good paint.  Add another $500-$1000 if you want low miles.<P>Or you could buy my nice-looking, good-running, never-wrecked 88 Coupe.  $2000 and it's yours!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
